-- title:
--   Integral q-expansions of the λ modular equation coefficients
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q \neq 2$ and a natural number $k$. Work inside the field $\mathbb{Q}((\mathfrak q))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ (Hahn series with integer exponents). Let $\lambda =$ `lambdaModC ℚ` be the image under the coefficientwise map $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Q}$ of the integral Laurent series `lambdaInt`, namely $\mathfrak q$ times the eighth power of the $\eta$-product series, times the substitution $\mathfrak q \mapsto \mathfrak q^{4}$ applied to its sixteenth power, times the substitution $\mathfrak q \mapsto \mathfrak q^{2}$ applied to the inverse $\eta$-unit series; and let `lambdaNModC ℚ q` be the result of applying to $\lambda$ the exponent-rescaling ring homomorphism $\mathfrak q \mapsto \mathfrak q^{q}$. Consider the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}(\lambda)$ of $\mathbb{Q}((\mathfrak q))/\mathbb{Q}$ generated by $\lambda$, and the minimal polynomial of `lambdaNModC ℚ q` over $\mathbb{Q}(\lambda)$. The assertion is that its $k$-th coefficient, viewed as an element of $\mathbb{Q}((\mathfrak q))$, satisfies `IntCoeffs`: for every $m \in \mathbb{Z}$ there is an integer $z$ with the $m$-th Laurent coefficient equal to $z$. The statement is for all $k$, the coefficients beyond the degree being $0$.
--
--   This is the integrality half of the level-two modular equation for Legendre's $\lambda$ (the classical relation between $\lambda(\mathfrak q)$ and $\lambda(\mathfrak q^{q})$, transposed from the level-one modular equation for $j$), the minimal polynomial having degree $q+1$ over $\mathbb{Q}(\lambda)$. It is used in the construction of the modular polynomial data attached to $\lambda$, in [`ModularCurve.nonempty_lambdaModularPolynomialData`](thm.html#ModularCurve.nonempty_lambdaModularPolynomialData).
